<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Provisional Central Government of Vietnam</h1><p class="st-meta">1948 CE – 1949 CE · capital Hanoi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A transitional French-backed Vietnamese government of 1948 to 1949, set up to prepare Bao Dai's return as head of state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> sought a non-communist alternative to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a>, it negotiated with former emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bao Dai</a>. In 1948 General Nguyen <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/early-ly-dynasty">Van Xuan</a>, president of the government of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-cochinchina">Cochinchina</a>, formed the Provisional Central Government of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-long-bay">Ha Long Bay</a> agreement of June 1948 recognised in principle the independence and unity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-fourth-republic">French Union</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The government had little real power: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> retained control of the army, finances and foreign relations, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> held much of the countryside. It merged the separate administrations in the north, centre and south, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/autonomous-republic-of-cochinchina">Autonomous Republic of Cochinchina</a>, into a single framework.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elysee-accords">Elysee Accords</a> of March 1949 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bao Dai</a> and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-auriol">Vincent Auriol</a> created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a>, with Bao Dai as head of state, replacing the provisional government that year.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/provisional-central-government-of-vietnam.jpg" alt="Provisional Central Government of Vietnam" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: (many, see history) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Vietnam.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1948 CE – 1949 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hanoi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nguyen Van Xuan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vietnamese, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chính phủ Trung ương Lâm thời Việt Nam</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1948&amp;focus=provisional-central-government-of-vietnam" aria-label="Provisional Central Government of Vietnam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7619 1775 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Provisional Central Government of Vietnam lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7939 2031L7939 2031M7939 2031L7939 2031"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7939 2031h0M7939 2031h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7939 2031h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7939 2031h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1948&amp;focus=provisional-central-government-of-vietnam">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
